---
import Artwork from "@/components/Artwork.astro"
import CampaignPartners from "@/components/CampaignPartners.astro"
import CampaignStatus from "@/components/CampaignStatus.astro"
import LegalRoute from "@/components/LegalRoute.astro"
import MetaPage from "@/components/MetaPage.astro"
import StoryScene from "@/components/StoryScene.astro"
import TimelineRail from "@/components/TimelineRail.astro"
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro"
import { campaignStatus } from "@/data/campaign"
import { homeSteps } from "@/data/history"
---

<Layout>
  <MetaPage slot="head" />
  <article class="campaign landing">
    <header class="hero">
      <div class="hero-text">
        <CampaignStatus language="en" />
        <h1>
          Financial privacy is a fundamental right.
          <span>Let’s defend it.</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="lead">
          You use your money to pay for medical treatment, support relatives or
          donate to a political cause. Financial privacy protects your freedom
          to do so without others scrutinising your personal life.
        </p>
        <nav class="hero-actions" aria-label="Get started">
          <a class="button primary" href="/en/get-involved/#spenden"
            >Support the case</a
          >
          <a class="button" href="/en/dossier/">What DAC8 requires</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
      <Artwork
        id="spendingProfile"
        language="en"
        size="hero"
        priority
        caption="What linked payments reveal. Not fields of the DAC8 report."
      />
    </header>
    <section class="brief" aria-label="DAC8 in Austria">
      <p class="brief-text">
        Since 2026, crypto-asset providers in Austria have had to prepare
        financial data on all their customers for the tax authority, without any
        specific suspicion.
        <strong
          >We believe this violates fundamental rights and are preparing a legal
          challenge.</strong
        >
      </p>
      <dl class="keyfigures">
        <div>
          <dt>
            <strong>Customers</strong>
            are covered, even if their data is not ultimately reported.
          </dt>
          <dd>All</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>
            <strong>Retention by the provider</strong>
            of records documenting checks and reporting of your data.
          </dt>
          <dd>10 <small>years</small></dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>
            <strong>First report</strong>
            to the tax authority, covering the 2026 reporting year.
          </dt>
          <dd><time datetime="2027-07-31">31 July 2027</time></dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </section>
    <section id="problem" aria-labelledby="problem-heading">
      <h2 id="problem-heading">What DAC8 does with your data</h2>
      <div class="story">
        <StoryScene
          art="accountToRecord"
          language="en"
          title="Your account becomes a file."
        >
          <p>
            You buy crypto-assets from an exchange or broker with reporting
            obligations in Austria. Since 2026, the provider has been preparing
            your identity and annual aggregates for the tax authority.
          </p>
        </StoryScene>
        <StoryScene
          art="massCapture"
          language="en"
          title="Everyone recorded. Without suspicion."
        >
          <p>
            Providers must prepare reporting data for all customers, including
            those who will not ultimately be reported. This requirement appears
            in Austrian law, not in the directive.
          </p>
        </StoryScene>
        <StoryScene
          art="reportAssets"
          language="en"
          title="What gets reported?"
          note="Pictured: a specimen for a natural person, not an official form."
          wide
          zoom
        >
          <p>
            Once a year: name, address, tax residence, tax identification number
            and date of birth, plus value in euros, units and, for most
            categories, the number of transactions for each crypto-asset and
            transaction type. Wallet addresses, individual transactions and your
            current balance are not included.
          </p>
        </StoryScene>
        <StoryScene
          art="yearsOnFile"
          language="en"
          title="More about you. Every year."
          note="Pictured: accumulating annual reports, not a central register."
        >
          <p>
            Annual reports reveal activity. Providers delete reported
            crypto-asset data ten years after the reporting period; for
            corresponding bank-account data, it is five.
          </p>
        </StoryScene>
      </div>
      <a class="text-link" href="/en/dossier/#gemeldet"
        >What is reported, and what is not</a
      >
    </section>
    <section id="risiko" aria-labelledby="risiko-heading">
      <h2 id="risiko-heading">What it can lead to</h2>
      <div class="story pair">
        <StoryScene
          art="dataHack"
          language="en"
          title="A data leak. Personal consequences."
          note="Pictured: linked provider records, not fields of the DAC8 report."
        >
          <p>
            A provider breach can expose the link between your name and
            blockchain transactions. Once that link spreads, it is hard to take
            back.
          </p>
        </StoryScene>
        <StoryScene
          art="rankedPortrait"
          language="en"
          title="Sortable by wealth. Dangerous in the wrong hands."
          note="Pictured: linked and leaked data, not fields of the DAC8 report."
        >
          <p>
            Total value and number of purchases give a rough estimate of how
            much money someone moves in crypto. In France, according to the
            Gendarmerie, criminals select victims using stolen customer data
            from a crypto tax service.
          </p>
        </StoryScene>
      </div>
      <a class="text-link" href="/en/dossier/#problem">Why it is a problem</a>
    </section>
    <section id="geschichte" aria-labelledby="geschichte-heading">
      <h2 id="geschichte-heading">How we got here</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">
        DAC8 is the eighth step of an EU directive whose predecessor dates from
        1977. Assistance in individual cases has turned into annual reports on
        salaries, bank accounts, platform sales and crypto-assets.
      </p>
      <TimelineRail language="en" steps={homeSteps} />
      <a class="text-link" href="/en/history/">The full history since 1977</a>
    </section>

    <section id="weg" aria-labelledby="weg-heading">
      <div class="art-split even">
        <div>
          <h2 id="weg-heading">What we are doing</h2>
          <p class="section-intro">
            <strong
              >We are convinced that the blanket collection of all customers’
              data violates fundamental rights.</strong
            >
          </p>
          <p class="prose">
            {campaignStatus.en.text}
            The authority and the courts decide the outcome.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Artwork id="collectionChallenge" language="en" caption={false} />
      </div>
      <LegalRoute language="en" />
      <a class="text-link" href="/en/proceedings/">Where the case stands</a>
    </section>
    <section id="handeln" aria-labelledby="handeln-heading">
      <h2 id="handeln-heading">How you can help</h2>
      <div class="act">
        <div class="act-lead">
          <Artwork id="collectiveDefense" language="en" caption={false} />
          <div>
            <h3>Help fund the case</h3>
            <p>
              Your donation primarily helps cover the costs of the proceedings.
            </p>
            <a class="button primary" href="/en/get-involved/#spenden"
              >Donate</a
            >
          </div>
        </div>
        <ul class="act-list">
          <li>
            <a href="/en/get-involved/#abgeordnete">
              <strong>Write to your MPs</strong>
              <span
                >A letter to the National Council, drafted in your browser and
                sent by you.</span
              >
            </a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/en/proceedings/">
              <strong>Follow the case</strong>
              <span>We publish news and next steps here and via RSS.</span>
            </a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="/en/get-involved/#teilen">
              <strong>Share the campaign</strong>
              <span
                >Ready-made graphics on the story, in German and English.</span
              >
            </a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section id="vision" class="vision" aria-labelledby="vision-heading">
      <h2 id="vision-heading" class="statement">
        Privacy needs no justification.
        <span>State interference does.</span>
      </h2>
      <p class="section-intro">
        A liberal democracy needs limits on state power. Specific suspicions of
        crime should be investigated in a targeted way, with a legal basis and
        independent oversight. Collecting everyone’s financial data as a
        precaution crosses a red line for us.
      </p>
      <div class="vision-points">
        <div>
          <h3>What we aim to achieve</h3>
          <ul class="checklist">
            <li>Overturn disproportionate obligations</li>
            <li>Limit data collection</li>
            <li>Secure effective protection for those affected</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="precedent">
          <h3>Why we are confident</h3>
          <p class="precedent-year" aria-hidden="true">2014</p>
          <div>
            <p>
              In 2014, the Court of Justice struck down the data retention
              directive and Austria’s Constitutional Court struck down the
              national law. That is why we combine concrete proceedings with
              political advocacy: in court and with the representatives who can
              change these laws.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="position" aria-labelledby="position-heading">
      <h2 id="position-heading">Who we are</h2>
      <div class="about">
        <div>
          <p class="section-intro">
            dac8.org is an initiative by Bitcoin Austria, the nonprofit Bitcoin
            association founded in Vienna in 2011. We are Bitcoiners and
            cypherpunks. We see privacy as a prerequisite for a free society.
          </p>
          <p class="prose">
            The campaign concerns everyone covered by DAC8, regardless of which
            crypto-assets they use. It starts in Austria, but DAC8 applies
            across the EU: if you want to challenge the law in your country, get
            in touch. We share arguments, sources and experience:
            <a href="mailto:kontakt@dac8.org">kontakt@dac8.org</a>
          </p>
        </div>
        <CampaignPartners />
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
